  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "Two travelling waves of equal amplitudes and equal frequencies move in opposite directions along a string. They interfere to produce a stationary wave whose equation is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>sin</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mi>cm</mi></math>. The amplitude of the particle at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> will be _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "5",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Amplitude is the maximum displacement from the mean position, for that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
